## Quality gates in code
- `gov.py analyze` — every machine-checkable clause of `shared/ARTIFACT-CONTRACTS.md` (EARS, traces, orphans, ID ownership/continuity, registry agreement, markers, manifest); a gate cannot open with a CRITICAL.
- `gov.py lint` — the constitution: profile schema, no removed concepts, no domain literals outside profiles, no stage/phase/prefix literals in code, generated files fresh.
- `python3 -m pytest {{ factory.paths.tools }}/tests -q` — the toolkit and orchestrator tests, every one against a temporary project repo the tests build themselves (plus a synthetic clinic profile, proving domain-agnosticism).

## Linking consumer repos
Edit the project's `{{ factory.project.file }} → repos` (url, checkout hints). Nothing is copied between repos: governance is written once into the project repo, which every consumer mounts as a submodule and pins. `paths.external` names the keys that resolve there — `{{ factory.paths.external['keys'] | join(', ') }}` — so every consumer reads the artifact where it was written. A track publishes into its own partition (the backend's api-docs into `{{ factory.project.partitions['api-docs'].path }}`) and reads its delivered packages from `{{ factory.project.partitions[factory.tracks.backend.delivery].path }}`; a frozen version is tagged `{{ factory.naming.tag }}` in the project repo.

Each consumer resolves through `CFG.repo_checkout()`: the env var named by its `checkout_env`, falling back to its `checkout_default` relative to the project checkout. Nothing in `governance-tools/` assumes a layout — don't special-case one when adding a repo link.
